    },
    {
      "id": "issue:TRNSPRTPCE-1003",
      "node_type": "issue",
      "label": "Issue TRNSPRTPCE-1003",
      "key": "TRNSPRTPCE-1003",
      "summary": "Path deletion can report success despite device failures",
      "status": "In Progress",
      "priority": "Medium",
      "issue_type": "Bug",
      "description": "Jira Issue TRNSPRTPCE-1003: Path deletion can report success despite device failures\nType: Bug | Status: In Progress | Priority: Medium\nComponents: None\nDescription: service-path  and  otn-service-path  deletion can report success when device configuration could not be read or removed. Failed cross-connect or interface reads are treated as missing resources, and a failed cross-connect delete commit is indistinguishable from a connection that did not exist. Clients cannot distinguish completed cleanup from cleanup requiring investigation or retry.\n \nFurther failure cases involve unknown or missing OpenROADM device versions, OpenROADM 7.1 interfaces with an absent or empty supporting-interface list ( NullPointerException  /  NoSuchElementException ), and unexpected runtime exceptions on individual nodes, which can stop cleanup of other nodes in the same request. Cleanup also performs redundant and separate device reads, including re-reading cross-connects it has just deleted.\n \nExpected outcome\n \nPath deletion reports incomplete cleanup as success=false with the affected node and reason, tolerates resources confirmed absent, and continues cleanup on other nodes where possible. Rollback records failures, and interrupt status is preserved.\n \nSupported OpenROADM 2.2.1 and 7.1 operations continue to dispatch normally.  Full service deletion retains its existing policy of proceeding despite device cleanup failures, including releasing the network-model resources it tracks.",
      "url": "https://lf-opendaylight.atlassian.net/browse/TRNSPRTPCE-1003",
      "project_id": "opendaylight/transportpce"
    },
    {
      "id": "issue:TRNSPRTPCE-1002",
      "node_type": "issue",
      "label": "Issue TRNSPRTPCE-1002",
      "key": "TRNSPRTPCE-1002",
      "summary": "get-pm reports success on failed reads and waits too long behind a busy device",
      "status": "In Progress",
      "priority": "Medium",
      "issue_type": "Bug",
      "description": "Jira Issue TRNSPRTPCE-1002: get-pm reports success on failed reads and waits too long behind a busy device\nType: Bug | Status: In Progress | Priority: Medium\nComponents: None\nDescription: When a device is frozen or unmounted, get-pm can return a successful response without measurements because read failures are represented as an empty PM list. A node without a supported OpenROADM version in port mapping also receives a successful empty response. Clients cannot distinguish either failure from a healthy device with no matching measurements.\n \nPM retrieval also needs a long read timeout because a healthy device may have a large current PM list. Applying that same allowance to waiting behind an existing device transaction causes requests that have not even started reading to occupy PM execution capacity for minutes. Several requests behind one frozen device can delay requests to healthy devices.\n \nExpected outcome\n \nFailed PM reads produce an application/operation-failed RPC error identifying the node and reason. Nodes without a supported port-mapping version produce application/data-missing without attempting a device read. A successful read containing no matching measurements remains successful.\n \nThe wait to acquire a device transaction can end substantially sooner than the allowed PM read duration. A request that cannot acquire the device in time reports a clear failure and releases its worker without disturbing transaction order. Large successful PM reads retain an adequate read allowance. Spanloss PM retrieval is outside this ticket.",
      "url": "https://lf-opendaylight.atlassian.net/browse/TRNSPRTPCE-1002",
      "project_id": "opendaylight/transportpce"
    },
    {
      "id": "issue:TRNSPRTPCE-1001",
      "node_type": "issue",
      "label": "Issue TRNSPRTPCE-1001",
      "key": "TRNSPRTPCE-1001",
      "summary": "Device transaction contention and closure failures can block unrelated work",
      "status": "In Progress",
      "priority": "Medium",
      "issue_type": "Bug",
      "description": "Jira Issue TRNSPRTPCE-1001: Device transaction contention and closure failures can block unrelated work\nType: Bug | Status: In Progress | Priority: Medium\nComponents: None\nDescription: Requests waiting for one busy device occupy workers shared by all devices. Interface deletion also holds a lock shared across nodes of the same OpenROADM version. As a result, an unresponsive node can prevent work on otherwise healthy nodes.\n \nTransaction closure has additional failure cases: a commit that throws before returning its future can leave the device held indefinitely, while concurrent commit and cancellation can release it before work has finished. Callers also lack a consistent way to bound transaction acquisition and distinguish failed reads from absent data.\n \nExpected outcome\n \nWaiting for one device does not prevent transaction creation or interface deletion on other devices. Transaction ownership remains exclusive and ordered through commit failures, timeouts and cancellation. Callers that need bounded acquisition and explicit read failures can obtain them without duplicating lifecycle handling. Existing callers must be considered when evolving the shared API.",
      "url": "https://lf-opendaylight.atlassian.net/browse/TRNSPRTPCE-1001",
      "project_id": "opendaylight/transportpce"
    },
    {
      "id": "issue:TRNSPRTPCE-1000",
      "node_type": "issue",
      "label": "Issue TRNSPRTPCE-1000",
      "key": "TRNSPRTPCE-1000",
      "summary": "Direct renderer service-delete fails when only a service name is supplied",
      "status": "In Progress",
      "priority": "Medium",
      "issue_type": "Bug",
      "description": "Jira Issue TRNSPRTPCE-1000: Direct renderer service-delete fails when only a service name is supplied\nType: Bug | Status: In Progress | Priority: Medium\nComponents: None\nDescription: The renderer service-delete RPC supplies a service name but passes no service object to  RendererServiceOperations.serviceDelete() . Once a path description is found, the renderer accesses the absent object's A-end and fails with NullPointerException  before device cleanup. The RPC wrapper can then return successful empty output, leaving the service undeleted without a useful failure response.\n \nExpected outcome\n \nA request naming an existing service can obtain the service details required for deletion. An absent service produces a clear failed response and notification. An interrupted lookup stops the request without losing interrupt status. Calls that already supply the service object remain supported.",
      "url": "https://lf-opendaylight.atlassian.net/browse/TRNSPRTPCE-1000",
      "project_id": "opendaylight/transportpce"
    },
    {
      "id": "issue:TRNSPRTPCE-999",
      "node_type": "issue",
      "label": "Issue TRNSPRTPCE-999",
      "key": "TRNSPRTPCE-999",
      "summary": "Slow device operations can exhaust RPC capacity and stall rendering",
      "status": "In Progress",
      "priority": "Medium",
      "issue_type": "Improvement",
      "description": "Jira Issue TRNSPRTPCE-999: Slow device operations can exhaust RPC capacity and stall rendering\nType: Improvement | Status: In Progress | Priority: Medium\nComponents: None\nDescription: Several TransportPCE RPCs wait for device operations on RESTCONF request threads. When devices respond slowly or stop responding, concurrent requests can occupy those threads and delay unrelated API calls. Renderer requests can also occupy every worker while waiting for subtasks scheduled on the same executor, preventing those subtasks from starting.\n \nSuggested solution\n \nKeep the API responsive while device work is pending, limit how much work  can accumulate, and let operators adjust capacity for their deployment.  Simply accepting more asynchronous requests would move the bottleneck  into an ever-growing work queue. Client cancellation and component shutdown also need defined behavior so unfinished work does not escape resource limits or leave callers waiting indefinitely.\n \nAffected RPCs\n \nservice-implementation-request service-delete (renderer), get-pm, service-path otn-service-path calculate-spanloss-base calculate-spanloss-current.\n \nExpected outcome\n \nDevice work does not hold RESTCONF request threads for its duration; outstanding work has finite limits; overload and shutdown are reported to clients with consistent RPC errors; execution capacity is configurable; and rendering requests can make progress without starving their own subtasks. This does not require an end-to-end deadline for every RPC.",
      "url": "https://lf-opendaylight.atlassian.net/browse/TRNSPRTPCE-999",
      "project_id": "opendaylight/transportpce"
    },
    {
      "id": "commit:de74a89",
      "node_type": "commit",
      "label": "Commit de74a89",
      "sha": "de74a89583abf7fcc7d8ce9188b552c8ff9792a4",
      "short_sha": "de74a89",
      "author": "Joakim T\u00f6rnqvist",
      "title": "Commit de74a89: TAPI - Reuse ROADM node builder in NBI converters",
      "message": "GitHub Commit de74a89 by Joakim T\u00f6rnqvist (2026-09-10T08:12:16+00:00)\nRepository: opendaylight/transportpce\nMessage: TAPI - Reuse ROADM node builder in NBI converters\n\nBuild the abstracted ROADM infrastructure nodes through the shared\nconversion helper instead of duplicating its builder logic. The\nduplicated code never set the error, loss, repeat-delivery,\ndelivery-order, unavailable-time, and server-integrity-process\ncharacteristics that the shared builder already fills in, so these\nNBI-exposed abstracted ROADM nodes were missing that data. Reusing the\nhelper fixes the gap and keeps cost/latency/risk characteristics\naligned with the other ROADM conversion paths.\n\nJIRA: TRNSPRTPCE-980\nChange-Id: I919dc154ac709d58ba2ec2f0b5d997f58c3c7829\nSigned-off-by: Joakim T\u00f6rnqvist <joakim.tornqvist@smartoptics.com>\nLinked Jira Issues: TRNSPRTPCE-980\nModified Files: tapi/src/main/java/org/opendaylight/transportpce/tapi/topology/ConvertTopoORtoTapiNbi.java, tapi/src/main/java/org/opendaylight/transportpce/tapi/topology/ConvertTopoTapiToTapiNbi.java",
      "timestamp": "2026-09-10T08:12:16+00:00",
      "url": "https://github.com/opendaylight/transportpce/commit/de74a89583abf7fcc7d8ce9188b552c8ff9792a4",
      "project_id": "opendaylight/transportpce"
    },
    {
